  "executive_briefing": "As of June 2026, the international tax and trade environment is marked by profound structural instability. Pillar Two (Global Minimum Tax) is undergoing a credibility crisis as major jurisdictions, notably the U.S., exhibit signs of unilateral withdrawal and legislative recalcitrance. This erosion of multilateral consensus has catalyzed a shift toward aggressive, high-frequency tariff regimes, forcing multinational enterprises to abandon long-term supply chain optimization in favor of real-time, tax-adjusted cost modeling. To compensate for lost corporate tax revenue predictability, national authorities\u2014led by regional hubs like Singapore\u2014are accelerating the mandate for real-time digital transaction reporting (e-invoicing) as the new enforcement frontier.",
